// A name a module's own source WRITES twice is an error, even where both stand
// for the same declaration: "if you've got two different ways of bringing in
// the same thing, that should be an error... it's a cleanliness issue" [Jon 23
// Sep]. What still merges is a name the module never wrote -- one a wildcard,
// an 'extends' or the implicit core import brought -- meeting another binding of
// the same declaration; module_fold_diamond runs those.
//
// Name-resolution stage. Two imports of one module, the one written duplicate
// decided at parse, are module_import_use_parse's.
//
// The folder holds:
//   c/c.cone             declares x and z
//   a/a.cone             re-exports c's names
//   b/b.cone             re-exports c's names too
//   twice/twice.cone     lists x twice in one clause
//   both/both.cone       lists x from c and again from a
//   thrice/thrice.cone   x by a wildcard, then listed from a, then listed from c
//   colors/colors.cone   'use Colors;' twice
//   meter/meter.cone     a module imported by name
//   relay/relay.cone     re-exports meter's name
//   gauge/gauge.cone     imports meter and lists 'meter' from relay
//   panel/panel.cone     a global's clause listing one member twice
//
// A module listing its own declaration, handed back by a module that extends
// it, is not here: the two would depend on each other, and imports form a DAG
// [Jon 23 Sep], so it is a loop, refused before any name folds.

mod module_fold_written_nameres;

fn reach() i64 {
  twice.t() + both.b() + thrice.th() + colors.red() + gauge.g() + panel.p();
}
